  "summary": "Mapping oscillatory regimes against synaptic, conduction, and modulatory parameters now gives testable coupling-level accounts across network scales.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that parameters governing coupling and modulation determine which oscillatory states a network can occupy. A balanced leaky integrate-and-fire study maps oscillatory regimes across synaptic decay, conduction delay, and plasticity rate using Brian2 simulations. A central pattern generator preprint reports that neuromodulation supports robust transitions between rhythmic patterns in degenerate networks with fixed connectivity, indicating that modulatory state can select among output patterns. A published E-I spiking study finds that sub-second cholinergic modulation desynchronizes intra-connected microcircuits, an effect absent under constant-tone modulation and dependent on modulation rate and connectivity. The items differ in model class and scale, so this thread is dominant rather than uniform. What is new is framing coupling at synaptic, population, and network levels as an explicit parameter space that can be mapped and tested.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
